0e89535380e33c9a232a5db7bb2841dec7a03b0fbe861b8c4.png) matrices ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/0dffbd47bb64c877dbb545c2c5024e2e50ce9ec516b7a4509d53f77916e8c791.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/c40e3405d51242d87b13698d1e715c1b391185890487e3d2a386cb09d3b952e9.png) we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/aae29aab049034fe50debd00d825e5e37b19de8f1a803dfb098727bbf8afe0e9.png)\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/bf7493e53cfed1ef296dcc0f7020d7ceec014af63a0cbee3f8cac5e1a11afd0d.png)\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we notice that it's upper triangular. Therefore, the determinant is simply the product of the diagonal entries.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/c23fa2f99aef303059a834682e511943ffbe022294cf2d9ac8e72e707316a607.png)st column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/b968eb1cf6b8a72d8f4b46202ea1a6ab201145ae2531bfe2b5a2049aafae9b28.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/example-4431.json" "file_sha256" "1efff9ef5774c70bae0858ac6fca07aad23fc7591f0dad36826d2dcae8dc171b"} {"question" "e-4432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $|A^{-1}|,$ if  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/8a5169c6a418a8ddaa9b3478e535039b253724ae622692ea728b2a94dc112e4a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/example-4432.json" "file_sha256" "591aa94f04fc2563cf1cd6e5bd203ade6ae501d2de0c66188cdfbcd3df3781b0"} {"question" "e-4432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|A^{-1}| = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 14 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-12+7) \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) =  \\dfrac{1}{|A|} = -\\dfrac{1}{15}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/example-4432.json" "file_sha256" "591aa94f04fc2563cf1cd6e5bd203ade6ae501d2de0c66188cdfbcd3df3781b0"} {"question" "e-4433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/96eacd1217f27ffb734d64453ac3fca3cac6124a781dcd677c898ec4a4317427.png)\nfind \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/64009b40661083c1e152b6277f329c6e22a0588fc0b3fd3d08db5e51ace0b27d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/example-4433.json" "file_sha256" "7bda69c1a14540dada500522cba1353356c992cb9f8ee32c55ff52965ae10ef5"} {"question" "e-4433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-10}+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/example-4433.json" "file_sha256" "7bda69c1a14540dada500522cba1353356c992cb9f8ee32c55ff52965ae10ef5"}] "usage" {"q-43063" [] "e-4431" [] "e-4433" [] "e-4432" [] "q-42581" [] "q-42584" []} "basis" 1739 "retractions" [[17592186120378 :question/problem "Find $det(A^{-1})$, if $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186120363 :question/problem "Find $\\mid A^{-1} \\mid$, if $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186120362 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$\\mid a + 3\\begin{vmatrix}-5 \\\\ b\\end{vmatrix}6|=-7$,\nfind\n$\\begin{vmatrix}a + 2 & -5 \\\\ b + 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}$."] [17592186119519 :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ find $\\det(A)$."] [17592186119519 :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $A$ and $B$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det(AB) = \\det(A)\\cdot \\det(B)\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -\\sqrt{11} \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we notice that it's upper triangular. Therefore, the determinant is simply the product of the diagonal entries.\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $1$st column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & -\\sqrt{11} \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (1 \\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot (-2(-1)^{1 + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= (-9) \\cdot ((-2) \\cdot (-5 + 4)) \\\\\n&= (-9) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&=-18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119520 :question/problem "Find $\\mid A^{-1} \\mid$, if $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 14 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119520 :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A^{-1} & = \\dfrac{1}{\\mid A\\end{vmatrix}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mid A \\mid$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & &= & 2 & 14\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\\\\n&= 7\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid \\\\\n&=-3(-1)^{3 + 2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (-12 + 7) \\\\\n&=-15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\mid A \\mid} = -\\dfrac{1}{15}\n$$"] [17592186119521 :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$\nfind\n$\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c + 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$"] [17592186119521 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c + 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &= \\begin{vmatrix}a + 0 & b + 0 & c + 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + 0 & b + 0 & c + 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}_(⏟)_(-10) +\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-10 + 1(-1)^{1 + 3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-10 - 1 \\\\\n&=-11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118648 17592186118649 17592186118650 17592186118651 17592186118652 17592186118654 17592186118655 17592186118656 17592186118657 17592186118658 17592186118744 17592186118745 17592186118746 17592186118747 17592186118748] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118647 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118647 :answer-field/id] [17592186118647 :answer-field/key] [17592186118647 :answer-field/type] [17592186118653 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118653 :answer-field/id] [17592186118653 :answer-field/key] [17592186118653 :answer-field/type] [17592186118743 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118743 :answer-field/id] [17592186118743 :answer-field/key] [17592186118743 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
